    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d409">

                <pb facs="0591" n="501" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg501"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.113/1557</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2774</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-06-07">June 7, 1934</date>.<lb/>[Received July
                        23.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Legation’s
                    despatch No. 2759, June 4, 1934,<anchor n="17" xml:id="d409-f1"/> in regard to
                    the export of arms and munitions of war to China, and to enclose for the
                    completion of the Department’s files copies of memoranda of conversations<anchor
                        n="18" xml:id="d409-f2"/> upon this subject.</p>
                <p>The Department’s attention is particularly invited to enclosure No. 6,<anchor
                        n="18" xml:id="d409-f3"/> being a memorandum of conversation between an
                    officer of this Legation and the First Secretary in charge of the Peiping office
                    of the Japanese Legation, from which it will be seen that the Japanese position
                    in the matter is that they will not acquiesce in what they call the Chinese
                    effort unilaterally to impose additional restrictions in a matter already
                    regulated by joint rules—the joint rules referred to being Rule III of the 1902
                        Tariff<anchor n="19" xml:id="d409-f4"/> and the 1908 regulations governing
                    the importation of arms and ammunition into China.<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d409-f5"/></p>
                <p>There is also enclosed a copy of a letter from Dr. Hsu Mo, Political <pb
                        facs="0592" n="502" xml:id="pg502"/> Vice Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs,<anchor n="21" xml:id="d409-f6"/> confirming previous assurances
                    that sporting arms and ammunition would not be affected by the regulations
                    governing the importation of munitions of war.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d409-f1" n="17" xml:id="d409-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it transmitted copies of Legation’s note to the Foreign Office and
                    instructions to consular officers in accordance with Department’s telegram No.
                    156, May 28, 8 p.m., <ref target="#pg499">p. 499</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d409-f2" n="18" xml:id="d409-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d409-f3" n="18" xml:id="d409-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d409-f4" n="19" xml:id="d409-f4-content">
                <p>Signed at Shanghai, September 6, 1902; see Annex III, “Treaty Between the United
                    States and China for the Extension of the Commercial Relations Between Them,”
                    and attached schedule of tariff duties, <ref target="frus1903#pg_100"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1903, pp. 100</ref>, <ref
                        target="frus1903#pg_101">101</ref>, <ref target="frus1903#pg_118"
                    >118</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d409-f5" n="20" xml:id="d409-f5-content">
                <p>Dated May 30, 1908, MacMurray, <hi rendition="#italic">Treaties</hi>, 1894–1919,
